 ## Overview
-Early Ray Termination (ERT) is an acceleration technique for image-order volume rendering that halts the front-to-back ray integration loop when the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby skipping the computation of voxels that are occluded by the foreground [S1][S5, S12, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods such as [Low Opacity Skipping](3608-low-opacity-skipping.md), which culls samples based on low local density, ERT culls based on global occlusion, exploiting the fact that once a ray becomes sufficiently opaque, subsequent samples contribute negligibly to the final pixel color [S1][S6, unofficial]. This method reduces memory bandwidth and arithmetic intensity by avoiding unnecessary traversal through the background or occluded regions of the volume [S15, unofficial].
+Early Ray Termination (ERT) is an acceleration technique for image-order volume rendering that halts the front-to-back ray integration loop when the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby skipping the computation of voxels that are occluded by the foreground [S1][S5, S12, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods such as [Low Opacity Skipping](3608-low-opacity-skipping.md), which culls samples based on low local density, ERT culls based on global occlusion, exploiting the fact that once a ray becomes sufficiently opaque, subsequent samples contribute negligibly to the final pixel color [S1][S6, unofficial]. This method reduces memory bandwidth by avoiding unnecessary traversal through the background or occluded regions of the volume [S15, unofficial].
 
